  "officialPoints": 20,
  "officialTimeMinutes": 36,
  "bodyHtml": "<p>Old Persian, one of the two attested Old Iranian languages, was spoken from 600-300 BCE in Achaemenid Persia. Old Persian was written in <em>cuneiform</em>, a writing system produced using wedge-shaped marks in clay tablets inherited from the Sumerian Empire.</p>\n<p>Below are some words written in Old Persian Cuneiform. Their transcriptions and English translations follow, in no particular order. Note that one word can be written in two different ways in cuneiform!</p>\n<figure><img src=\"/content-assets/2020-open-C-cuneiform-words.png\" alt=\"Twenty-six numbered Old Persian cuneiform words arranged in two columns, numbered 1 to 26\" /></figure>\n<p>Here are the transcriptions and English translations of the words above, in no particular order. Note that y, š and č are consonants; ā, ī, and ū are long versions of their corresponding vowels (a, i, and u). Capitalization in transcription is not reflected in the Persian script.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\">\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Transcription</th><th>Translation</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A.</td><td><span class=\"data\">paruvam</span></td><td>previously</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B.</td><td><span class=\"data\">hadugā</span></td><td>record, statue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C.</td><td><span class=\"data\">āyadana</span></td><td>sanctuary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D.</td><td><span class=\"data\">dāruv</span></td><td>wood</td></tr>\n<tr><td>E.</td><td><span class=\"data\">duruxta</span></td><td>lie</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Čišpaiš</span></td><td>Tespes (a king of Persia)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G.</td><td><span class=\"data\">saiymam</span></td><td>silver</td></tr>\n<tr><td>H.</td><td><span class=\"data\">bar</span></td><td>to bear</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Skudra</span></td><td>Thrace</td></tr>\n<tr><td>J.</td><td><span class=\"data\">baga</span></td><td>god</td></tr>\n<tr><td>K.</td><td><span class=\"data\">pasāva</span></td><td>after</td></tr>\n<tr><td>L.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Ākaufačiyā</span></td><td>a tribe of southeastern Iran</td></tr>\n<tr><td>M.</td><td><span class=\"data\">radīy</span></td><td>because of</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Kuruš</span></td><td>Cyrus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>O.</td><td><span class=\"data\">asmā</span></td><td>sky</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Bagadāta</span></td><td>(a male name)*</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Q.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Hiduš</span></td><td>India</td></tr>\n<tr><td>R.</td><td><span class=\"data\">hača</span></td><td>from</td></tr>\n<tr><td>S.</td><td><span class=\"data\">bājim</span></td><td>tribute, toll</td></tr>\n<tr><td>T.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Arabāya</span></td><td>Arabia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>U.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Haraiva</span></td><td>(a female name)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>V.</td><td><span class=\"data\">daiva</span></td><td>false god</td></tr>\n<tr><td>W.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Ūvja</span></td><td>Elam (a place name)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>X.</td><td><span class=\"data\">hakaramčīy</span></td><td>once</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Y.</td><td><span class=\"data\">Dārayauš</span></td><td>Darius (a king of Persia)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>* Equivalent to the Greek name <em>Theodoros</em> (“God-given”)</p>",
