  "hints": [
    {
      "tier": 1,
      "text": "Do not try to identify individual wedges yet. Start by counting: how many separate signs does each cuneiform word contain, and how many syllables (consonant plus vowel groups) does each transcription contain? The shortest cuneiform words and the shortest transcriptions have to belong together.",
      "xpCost": 1
    },
    {
      "tier": 2,
      "text": "Old Persian cuneiform is mostly syllabic rather than alphabetic: most signs stand for a consonant plus a vowel (like ba, ku, ra), and there are also a few plain vowel signs. A long vowel is usually written by adding an extra vowel sign after the syllable sign. Once you have matched a couple of short words, you know the value of individual signs, and you can hunt for those same signs inside the longer words to pin them down.",
      "xpCost": 2
    },
    {
      "tier": 3,
      "text": "Here is the counting method on a made-up word. If the language had a word kama, it would need just two signs, ka and ma. The word kama with a long first vowel, kama with the a written long, would need three: ka, then the vowel sign a, then ma. So a cuneiform word with three signs can correspond either to a three-syllable transcription or to a two-syllable transcription that contains one long vowel. Keep both options open and use repeated signs, which mark repeated syllables, to break the tie.",
      "xpCost": 3
    }
  ],
  "explanationHtml": "<h3>What kind of writing system is this?</h3>\n<p>Old Persian cuneiform looks intimidating, but it is not a picture-writing system. Almost every sign stands for a <em>syllable</em>: a consonant followed by a vowel (ba, ku, ra, and so on). There are also three plain vowel signs (a, i, u). So a written word is roughly a string of syllables, and the number of signs you see is close to the number of syllables you hear.</p>\n<p>That single fact turns the puzzle into arithmetic before it turns into decipherment.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: count signs, count syllables</h3>\n<p>Go through the cuneiform list and write down how many signs each word has. Then go through the transcriptions and count the consonant-plus-vowel groups. For example <span class=\"data\">hača</span> breaks up as ha-ča, two syllables; <span class=\"data\">Kuruš</span> breaks up as ku-ru-š(a), three signs, because a final consonant still has to be written with a syllable sign; <span class=\"data\">Ākaufačiyā</span> is long no matter how you slice it and must be one of the longest cuneiform words.</p>\n<p>Two extra rules make the counts line up:</p>\n<ul><li>A <strong>long vowel</strong> (ā, ī, ū) is normally written by adding a plain vowel sign after the syllable sign: <span class=\"data\">bā-</span> takes two signs, ba plus a.</li><li>A <strong>word-final consonant</strong> is written with a syllable sign whose vowel is simply not pronounced, so it still costs one sign.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: use the very short and very long words as anchors</h3>\n<p>The extremes are the safest. The shortest cuneiform words can only be the shortest transcriptions, and the single longest cuneiform word can only be the longest transcription. Match those first, even if you are not yet sure which sign is which. Each match you make hands you the sound values of two or three signs for free.</p>\n<h3>Step 3: reuse the signs you have learned</h3>\n<p>Now the puzzle becomes a crossword. Suppose you have decided that a two-sign word is <span class=\"data\">baga</span> (god). You now know the sign for ba and the sign for ga. Look for a longer word that begins with those same two signs: the transcription list contains <span class=\"data\">Bagadāta</span>, literally “god-given”, which must begin with exactly that pair. The Greek footnote (<em>Theodoros</em>, “God-given”) is a nudge that this compound really does contain the word for god.</p>\n<p>The same trick works with repeated signs. A word whose first and second signs are identical must have two identical syllables in a row, which narrows the candidates dramatically. A word containing a plain vowel sign in the middle almost certainly has a long vowel there.</p>\n<h3>Step 4: the trap in the instructions</h3>\n<p>There are 26 cuneiform words but only 25 transcriptions, and the problem warns you that “one word can be written in two different ways in cuneiform.” In Old Persian, spelling really was not fully standardised: a scribe could sometimes choose whether to write a vowel out with an extra sign or leave it implied. So exactly one letter of the answer key is used twice, and if every letter you have used is unique you know you still have work to do.</p>\n<h3>Step 5: proper names are gifts</h3>\n<p>Names such as Cyrus, Darius, India, Arabia, Thrace and Elam are recognisable because their syllable shapes are unusual: they contain sound sequences that the ordinary vocabulary words do not. Names also tend to be longer, which again shows up in the sign count.</p>\n<h3>Why the answers come out where they do</h3>\n<p>Working from the extremes inwards, and checking each new match against the signs you have already identified, the whole grid closes up: the two-sign items go to the two-syllable words, the seven- and eight-sign items go to the tribe name and the long personal names, and the leftover letter that appears twice is the one the introduction warned you about. The key insight worth remembering is that you never had to know in advance what any wedge shape meant. Counting signs and comparing shapes across words gave you the sound values from inside the data.</p>",
